# Windshift CLI

This project is connected to Windshift workspace **{{.Workspace.Key}}** ({{.Workspace.Name}}).

## Quick Commands

```bash
# My work
ws task mine              # Tasks assigned to me
ws task created           # Tasks I created

# Create & manage
ws task create -t "Title" [-d "Description"]
ws task move <KEY-123> <status>
ws task get <KEY-123>

# Test execution
ws test run mine          # My test runs
ws test run start <set>   # Start test run
ws test result <run> <case> passed|failed|blocked|skipped

# Pages (specs, design docs, runbooks — workspace documentation)
ws page list                       # Check for an existing spec / doc first
ws page get <id>                   # Stream a page as Markdown
ws page create --file spec.md      # Publish a new spec / design / runbook
ws page edit <id> --file spec.md   # Update an existing page

# Links (cross-entity: item ↔ item / item ↔ page / item ↔ test_case)
ws link add WI-1 page:42           # Auto-picks the Page link type
ws link ls WI-1                    # Outgoing + incoming links on WI-1
ws link rm <link-id>               # Remove a link by id
ws link types                      # Catalog of available link types
```

## Referencing items in commits

When the work belongs to a Windshift ticket, include the item key in the
**commit subject line** so `git log --oneline` and PR titles surface the
linkage at a glance. The parenthetical-suffix form pairs well with
Conventional Commits:

```
feat(v1): expose item labels + diagrams on the bearer-token surface ({{.Workspace.Key}}-71)
fix(links): show workspace_item_number in linked-item key, not DB id ({{.Workspace.Key}}-58)
```

Look the key up via `ws task get` or `ws task mine` if it isn't already
in the conversation. If a commit closes multiple tickets, list each key.
Small fixes / tooling cleanups that aren't tracked under a ticket may
omit the key — don't fabricate one.
